Dear Roger

Many thanks for this (and indirectly to Dan, too).

> Ah, yes, then vlength is within-target.

> Also, I hadn't noticed this before, but Dan Johnson pointed out to me that you actually have interactions of 
> vlength & dialect as fixed effects in your model, with each other and with other variables.  Be sure to 
> interpret the main effects with care given that they are involved in higher-order interactions in your model.  If 
> those interactions are of theoretical importance, then you may want to consider random slopes for them.

Yes - in fact my key prediction is of an interaction of dialect and vlength. So yes, I should include a random slope for that interaction -- presumably (1+dialect*vlength|target) ? (The corresponding random slope for subjects isn't possible, because dialect is between-subjects.)

Could I ask a couple of more general (and, sorry, still quite basic) questions? 

1) I'm still wrestling with how to ensure I've arrived at the best model in terms of both random and fixed effects. (As my models are taking 30 minutes+ each to run, and I have a four-way interaction in my starting model, and also have four dependent variables to analyse, I'm particularly keen to have a sensible plan before beginning.) From what I've read, I believe I should start from a full model (all main effects and interactions) for both the fixed and random effects. This won't, I think, converge, and will also contain a number of nonsignificant predictors, so I will need to simplify it, but in which order should that be done? Should I simplify the random effects structure first, one term at a time, until the model both converges and contains only justified predictors; and after that,
  move on to simplifying the fixed effects in similar fashion? Or should I remove a given term first from the random effects, then the fixed effects, before moving on to the next term?

2) Roger writes:
> Be sure to 
> interpret the main effects with care given that they are involved in higher-order interactions in your model.
Yes - I usually deal with this by relevelling and refitting to ensure that I've extracted all the relevant contrasts. However, in this case, I don't think this will get me the comparisons I need with respect to vlength. Vlength interacts with dialect, but also with context and vtype (individually and together) while dialect does not interact with either context or vtype. I *think* this means that the estimates and p-values for vlength are all going to with respect to particular reference levels of context and vtype. But to interpret the dialect:vlength interaction, what I want is to pool across the levels of context and vtype, in order to establish whether the levels of vlength differ for each level of dialect. So I presume that I'll need a different type of contrast coding here?

Thanks again for your help -- I clearly have a lot to learn here.

Last but not least, I'll keep the list posted as to whether I get any repeats of the weird result from model comparison, which motivated my original post, once I've got the random effects structure right.

Best

Rachel

